## Fragility Measurements
The fictive temperature, $T_{\mathfrak{f}},$ of a glass (i.e. in the nonliquid state) may be thought of as the temperature at which the structure of an equilibrium liquid is frozen in.33 The dependence of $T_{\mathfrak{f}}$ upon the heating rate used in the DSC scan $(q_{\mathsf{h}})$ (Fig. S5), allows determination of the kinetic fragility index, $m,^{\bar{19},33,34}$ which quantifies the speed of the dynamical processes of a liquid as it approaches the glass transition temperature. Interestingly, despite $\mathrm{ZIF-}_{4}$ and $\operatorname{Zn}(\operatorname{Im})_{2}$ (GIS) possessing the same chemical formula and melting at the same temperature, the liquids produced exhibit very different fragilities $\langle m=39$ and 17 respectively, Fig. 3). The differences here were confirmed by evaluation of $m$ for $a_{\top}Z\mathbf{n}(\mathrm{Im})_{2}$ (GIS) $(m=15)$ , which lies close to that of $a_{\mathfrak{g}}\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{Im})_{2}$ (GIS) (Fig. $S_{7}\mathrm{\overline{{{\Omega}}}}$ ). This is consistent with the noticeably similar fragilities of $a_{\mathfrak{g}}{\mathrm{ZIF-}}4$ ( $\mathbf{\bar{\rho}}_{m}$ ${\bf\Omega}=39,$ ) and $a_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathsf{T}}\mathrm{ZIF}{-}_{4}$ $(m=41)$ ).
The different fragilities of the liquids formed upon melting of ZIF- $_4$ and $\operatorname{Zn}(\operatorname{Im})_{2}$ (GIS) may stem from a remnant influence of the network architecture of the solid phase, given the relatively small window between melting and quenching of the samples. Indeed, whilst chemically identical, the phases immediately prior to melting are not the same (ZIF-zni and $a_{\scriptscriptstyle\top}\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{Im})_{2}$ (GIS) respectively). Fragility was also observed to decrease on going from ZIF- $^4$ $m=$ 39) to ZIF-62 $(m=35)$ and then to $\mathrm{TIF}{\cdot}4$ $(m=23)$ ), and hence, for those frameworks displaying the cag topology, fragility decreases with increasing linker content and size.
Whilst an increase in fragility with increasing linker size may have been expected based on free volume considerations and the increase in $T_{\mathrm{g}},$ similar changes in the fragility index of other glass-forming polymers have been noted,31,35 and ascribed to the relative side group stiffness compared to the polymer backbone. The lower kinetic fragilities of the $\operatorname{Zn}(\operatorname{Im})_{2}$ (GIS) and TIF- $^{\cdot}4$ melts compared to the ZIF- $^{\cdot}4$ melt are consistent with smaller $C_{\mathfrak{p}}$ increases during the glass transition, given that the latter is a measure of thermodynamic fragility.20
